In a given figure, a 4-inch by 4-inch square is attached to a 12-inch by 12-inch square. Determine the area of the shaded region formed similarly as described in the original problem. [asy]
unitsize(2mm);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7pt)+fontsize(12pt));

pair H=(0,0), A=(0,12), B=(12,12), C=(12,4), G=(12,0), E=(16,4), F=(16,0);
pair D=extension(A,F,B,G);

draw(A--B--G--H--cycle);
fill(D--C--E--F--cycle, black);
draw(A--F);
draw(G--F--E--C);

label("$A$",A,NW);
label("$H$",H,SW);
label("$B$",B,NE);
label("$C$",C,NE);
label("$G$",G,S);
label("$D$",D,WSW);
label("$E$",E,NE);
label("$F$",F,SE);
[/asy]

A paper equilateral triangle $ABC$ has side length 15. The paper triangle is folded so that vertex $A$ touches a point on side $\overline{BC}$ a distance 4 from point $C$. Find the square of the length of the line segment along which the triangle is folded.

[asy]
import cse5;
size(12cm);
pen tpen = defaultpen + 1.337;
real a = 39/5.0;
real b = 39/7.0;
pair B = MP("B", (0,0), dir(200));
pair A = MP("A", (11,0), dir(-80));
pair C = MP("C", (15,0), dir(-20));
pair K = (7.5,12.990);
pair M = (a*B+(15-a)*K) / 15;
pair N = (b*C+(15-b)*K) / 15;
draw(B--M--N--C--cycle, tpen);
fill(M--A--N--cycle, mediumgrey);
draw(M--A--N--cycle);
pair shift = (-20.73, 0);
pair B1 = MP("B", B+shift, dir(200));
pair A1 = MP("A", K+shift, dir(90));
pair C1 = MP("C", C+shift, dir(-20));
draw(A1--B1--C1--cycle, tpen);[/asy]

Rational Man and Hyperbolic Man both decide to test their new cars on different tracks. Rational Man drives on a circular track parameterized by:
\begin{align*}
x &= \cos t, \\
y &= \sin t,
\end{align*}
Hyperbolic Man drives on a path parameterized by:
\begin{align*}
x &= -1 + 3 \cos \frac{t}{2}, \\
y &= 5 \sin \frac{t}{2}.
\end{align*}
Determine the smallest possible distance $AB$ between a point $A$ on Rational Man's track and a point $B$ on Hyperbolic Man's track.

